---
import EditorialHeader from "../../components/EditorialHeader.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const repository = "https://github.com/dougborg/ends-and-means";
const correctionUrl = `${repository}/issues/new?title=Correction%3A+%5Bpage+or+claim%5D&body=Page+or+claim%3A%0A%0AWhat+should+change%3A%0A%0AEvidence+and+exact+passage%3A`;
const reconsiderationUrl = `${repository}/issues/new?title=Reconsideration%3A+%5Bdecision+or+page%5D&body=Decision+or+page%3A%0A%0AReason+for+reconsideration%3A%0A%0ANew+evidence+or+overlooked+policy%3A`;
const privateSecurityUrl = `${repository}/security/advisories/new`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Editorial governance" description="Who is accountable for Ends and Means, and how corrections, appeals, conflicts, sensitive material, and AI-assisted work are handled." fullBleed>
  <article class="editorial-page governance-page">
    <EditorialHeader coordinate="EDITORIAL GOVERNANCE" title="Who decides, and how can a decision change?">
      <p class="dek measure-standfirst">Ends and Means is a small, independently maintained project. A person is accountable for every publication decision, and evidence-backed disagreement can lead to correction or reconsideration.</p>
    </EditorialHeader>

    <nav class="section-jump" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="coordinate">ON THIS PAGE</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#responsibility">Responsibility</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></li>
        <li><a href="#appeals">Reconsideration</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sensitive-subjects">Sensitive subjects</a></li>
        <li><a href="#independence">Independence</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ai">AI-assisted work</a></li>
        <li><a href="#privacy">Privacy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#changes">Policy changes</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section class="model-boundary" id="responsibility" aria-labelledby="responsibility-title">
      <p class="coordinate">RESPONSIBILITY</p>
      <h2 id="responsibility-title">The project editor holds final authority and final responsibility.</h2>
      <p>Contributors can propose evidence and wording. Reviewers test source fitness, interpretation, fairness, and presentation. The project editor decides what is published and remains answerable for the framing, wording, and decision reasons; a contributor, tool, or source does not inherit that responsibility.</p>
      <p>No change is accepted only because a contributor is prominent, a view is popular, or an automated check passes. Structural checks must pass, but a person decides whether the evidence supports the claim and whether the treatment is fair.</p>
      <p>If the editor has a personal, financial, professional, or close advocacy conflict, it is disclosed and the editor does not perform the substantive review. An independent reviewer should recommend an outcome. If no suitable reviewer is available, publication or reconsideration waits rather than presenting a conflicted judgment as independent. The editor still records the final accountable decision.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-records" id="corrections" aria-labelledby="corrections-title">
      <header>
        <p class="coordinate">CORRECTIONS</p>
        <h2 id="corrections-title">What happens after a correction is suggested?</h2>
        <p><a href={correctionUrl}>Send a correction</a> with the affected page or claim, the proposed change, and a source with an exact passage when possible. The form is public; do not include private or sensitive personal information.</p>
      </header>
      <div>
        <section><h3>1. Received</h3><p>The editor checks that the concern is understandable and requests missing information when needed. The project aims to acknowledge a usable submission within seven days, but this is a target, not a guarantee.</p></section>
        <section><h3>2. Assessed</h3><p>The claim, cited passage, contrary evidence, scope, and likely harm are reviewed. A correction does not need to share the project’s conclusion, but it must identify a checkable factual, evidentiary, interpretive, or policy concern.</p></section>
        <section><h3>3. Decided</h3><p>The outcome is accepted, accepted in part, declined, or held for more evidence. The public reason identifies the governing evidence or policy and the material limitation. The project aims to give an outcome or a progress update within thirty days.</p></section>
        <section><h3>4. Published or closed</h3><p>A routine accepted correction publishes after source and quality checks. An urgent error may be removed or narrowly corrected before the full review finishes when leaving it visible could cause material harm; the reason and durable correction follow as soon as practical.</p></section>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="model-boundary" id="appeals" aria-labelledby="appeals-title">
      <p class="coordinate">RECONSIDERATION</p>
      <h2 id="appeals-title">A decision can be challenged with new evidence or an overlooked rule.</h2>
      <p>An appeal is handled as a request for reconsideration. <a href={reconsiderationUrl}>Request reconsideration</a> by identifying the decision and explaining the material evidence, reasoning, conflict, or policy that may have been missed. Repeating the original submission without a specific error or new basis may be closed with that reason.</p>
      <p>Someone other than the original substantive reviewer should assess the request when an eligible person is available. In this small project that may not always be possible; the editor will disclose that limitation, review the decision afresh, and explain the result, or defer it until independent review is available. One reconsideration is normally final unless materially new evidence appears.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="research-notes" id="sensitive-subjects" aria-labelledby="sensitive-title">
      <h2 id="sensitive-title">Which subjects receive heightened care?</h2>
      <section><h3>Living people</h3><p>Claims that could materially affect a living person require strong, directly relevant sourcing, careful attribution, and a clear public-interest reason. Unnecessary personal details, speculation about motives, and disproportionate repetition of allegations are excluded. Credible safety, privacy, and accuracy concerns receive urgent review.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Communities and Indigenous peoples</h3><p>The project seeks community self-description and community-authored or oral-history evidence where appropriate, records who supplied and authorized that knowledge, and distinguishes it from government, colonial, translated, or outside scholarly labels. A community is not treated as internally uniform. Requests about names, consent, restricted knowledge, present-day risk, and who has authority to speak receive substantive review rather than cosmetic relabeling.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Contested and harmful terms</h3><p>Historical or harmful language appears only when identification, quotation, or analysis requires it. The page explains who used the term, in what setting, and why it matters; the project does not silently adopt a slur, imposed identity, or evolutionary category as its own classification. Searchability does not override dignity or safety.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Contentious history</h3><p>Accounts distinguish documented events, participant claims, later interpretations, and current disputes. Harm is neither sensationalized nor hidden to make a system look cleaner. Source survival, state archives, translation, and unequal power to leave records are treated as limits on what can be concluded.</p></section>
    </section>

    <section class="model-boundary" id="independence" aria-labelledby="independence-title">
      <p class="coordinate">INDEPENDENCE AND MODERATION</p>
      <h2 id="independence-title">Evidence is considered without a commercial or personal veto.</h2>
      <p>Sponsors, donors, advertisers, and affiliate relationships cannot select sources, suppress findings, choose classifications, or shape conclusions. A relevant relationship is disclosed beside the affected work. The project does not currently sell favorable coverage; any future commercial arrangement must preserve this rule.</p>
      <p>Evidence-backed disagreement, forceful criticism, and challenges to editorial assumptions are welcome. Harassment, threats, dehumanizing attacks, doxxing, spam, coordinated disruption, and repeated bad-faith demands are not. Material may be limited or removed to protect people and keep discussion usable; the moderation reason should be stated without republishing abuse or private information.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-records" id="ai" aria-labelledby="ai-title">
      <header>
        <p class="coordinate">AI-ASSISTED WORK</p>
        <h2 id="ai-title">AI can assist the work, but it is never evidence or authority.</h2>
        <p>Automation may help discover candidates, compare records, draft prose, or flag possible problems. Its output cannot support a claim and cannot decide what is fair, accurate, or ready to publish.</p>
      </header>
      <div>
        <section><h3>Discovery and synthesis</h3><p>A person opens and evaluates every cited source, checks the exact passage, seeks serious alternatives, and decides whether it fits the claim. An AI summary is a lead, not provenance.</p></section>
        <section><h3>Drafting and checking</h3><p>A person remains responsible for framing, interpretation, wording, fairness, source similarity, and publication. Machine-generated prose receives the same source and originality review as any other draft.</p></section>
        <section><h3>Automated signals</h3><p>Checks can detect missing fields, broken relationships, malformed citations, or similar strings. These are attention signals. They do not determine plagiarism, truth, source quality, identity, balance, or harm.</p></section>
        <section><h3>Privacy and disclosure</h3><p>Private submissions, restricted community knowledge, and unnecessary personal data must not be sent to external AI services. Material AI assistance is disclosed when it helps readers understand how an explanation was produced or limited; routine spelling and structural checks do not require a label.</p></section>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="model-boundary" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
      <p class="coordinate">PRIVACY, CONSENT, AND RECORDS</p>
      <h2 id="privacy-title">Collect less, attribute with permission, and state what cannot be erased.</h2>
      <p>Public submissions should contain only information needed to evaluate the proposed change. A contributor is credited only with permission; otherwise the public record may describe the evidence without naming the submitter. Consent to be named can be withdrawn for future display, though copies already distributed and repository history may persist.</p>
      <p>Public discussion and revision history are retained as long as they remain part of the project record. Unnecessary personal data, doxxing, or material published without valid consent may be redacted from the site and active discussion. Because public history and third-party copies may remain, the project cannot promise complete deletion. Security vulnerabilities belong in the <a href={privateSecurityUrl}>private security report</a>, not the public correction form.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="model-boundary" id="changes" aria-labelledby="changes-title">
      <p class="coordinate">POLICY CHANGES AND EXCEPTIONS</p>
      <h2 id="changes-title">The policy can change, but not silently.</h2>
      <p>Material amendments receive the same public, human-reviewed change history as editorial content. A change should explain the problem, the new rule, and the trade-off. It applies prospectively unless the reason for reviewing earlier material is stated.</p>
      <p>An exception must identify the rule, the bounded circumstance, who approved it, why ordinary treatment would cause greater harm or undermine accuracy, and when the exception ends or is reviewed. Convenience, schedule pressure, and contributor status are not sufficient reasons.</p>
      <p>For implementation detail, researchers may inspect the optional <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/.agents/skills/research-content-changes/SKILL.md`}>research protocol</a> and <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/.agents/skills/coordinate-project-delivery/SKILL.md`}>review protocol</a>. Those technical instructions support this policy; they do not replace the public commitments above.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
